// ATT&CK: lateral-movement, command-and-control, T1090
// Description: Detects the modification of the PortProxy registry key which is used for port forwarding.
// False positives:
//   - WSL2 network bridge PowerShell script used for WSL/Kubernetes/Docker (e.g. https://github.com/microsoft/WSL/issues/4150#issuecomment-504209723)
//   - Synergy Software KVM (https://symless.com/synergy)
// References:
//   - https://www.fireeye.com/blog/threat-research/2019/01/bypassing-network-restrictions-through-rdp-tunneling.html
//   - https://adepts.of0x.cc/netsh-portproxy-code/
//   - https://www.dfirnotes.net/portproxy_detection/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ad147b5514596e70f2782769d9833046b7c8e12b/rules/windows/registry/registry_event/registry_event_portproxy_registry_key.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.
// Note: 1 wildcard comparison(s) rewritten to 'matches regex' (KQL treats * in contains/endswith/== as a literal character)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey matches regex @"(?i)\\Services\\PortProxy\\v4tov4\\tcp.*$"
